Logic :

Initialize i as 1 and find the length of the array.
Traverse the array using a while loop to place each number at its correct index.
Check if the current element is already at its correct position (arr[i - 1] == i).
If the element is not in its correct position, find its correct index using arr[i - 1] - 1.
Check whether the correct position already contains the same value.
If the same value is already present, it means that number is duplicated, so move to the next index.
Otherwise, swap the current element with the element at its correct position.
Repeat this process until all numbers are placed in their correct positions.
Traverse the array again and compare each element with its expected value (index + 1).
If an element does not match its expected position, that element is the duplicate number and the missing number is the index value (j + 1).
Return an array containing the duplicate number and the missing number.
If no mismatch is found, return {-1, -1}.

Code :

class Solution {
    public int[] findErrorNums(int[] arr) {
        int i=1;
        int len=arr.length;
        
        while(i<=len){

             if(arr[i-1]!=i){
                int temp = arr[i-1];
               if(arr[i-1] == arr[temp-1]){
                i++;
               }
               else{
                   arr[i-1] = arr[temp-1];
                   arr[temp-1] = temp;
               }

             }
       
            else {
                 i++;
            }
        }
            
            for(int j=0;j<arr.length;){
                if(arr[j]==j+1){
                    
                    j++;
                    
              }
               
                    else{
                         return new int[] {arr[j],j+1};
                    }


            }
            return new int[] {-1,-1};         
 
        
    }
}
